1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,010
Listen to a talk in an architecture class.

2
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:06,820
W: The Eastgate Center in Harare

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:12,030
Zimbabwe, is a mixing complex combining retail spaces and offices.

4
00:00:12,030 --> 00:00:17,240
When the architect Mick Pierce was asked to design the building in the early 1990s

5
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:19,090
he faced a big challenge.

6
00:00:19,090 --> 00:00:21,241
How to keep all its shops

7
00:00:21,241 --> 00:00:23,740
and offices cool without traditional air conditioning

8
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,260
and do it during an economic crisis.

9
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:32,090
Energy costs were high, and importing advanced air conditioners was impossible.

10
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:36,120
To solve this dilemma Pierce looked to termite mounds.

11
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:39,210
Termites are insects that live in large colonies.

12
00:00:39,210 --> 00:00:43,170
They build big mounds on the ground throughout the African savannah.

13
00:00:43,170 --> 00:00:48,240
The mounds are amazing structures, summarizing 3 m above ground

14
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:51,240
and extend another one to 3 m below the ground.

15
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:56,570
The underground chambers connect to the above-ground chimneys through a maze of tunnels,

16
00:00:56,570 --> 00:01:00,260
and this entire system works like natural climate control.

17
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:06,110
Warm air rises and escapes through the top, while cooler air flows in from below.

18
00:01:06,110 --> 00:01:12,120
The thick walls of soil and clay absorb heat during the day and release it at night.

19
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:16,360
Termites fine-tune airflow by opening and closing vents

20
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:20,210
adding or removing soil to enlarge or seal passages.

21
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:23,240
On hot days, they open more vents for cooling.

22
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:26,260
At night, they close them to keep warm.

23
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:29,120
Then Pierce borrowed these ideas.

24
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:33,639
The Eastgate Center's walls are made from thick materials like concrete and brick,

25
00:01:33,639 --> 00:01:35,840
and there's a system of ducts

26
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:39,200
and adjustable louvers throughout the building to mimic those vents.

27
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:46,060
The result, a building that uses far less energy than its conventional counterparts.
